  from the DOM and renders the error messages. Worth being deliberate, because the duplication across forms isn't only
  the rule logic; look at [38;5;153mapp.js:17-28[39m and most of what a second form would copy is the submit-handler boilerplate: grab
  each field by id, call validate, branch, report the error.

  - [1mPure core only[0m — [38;5;153mvalidate(data, schema)[39m returns per-field errors. Each form reads its own inputs and displays its
    own errors. Trivially testable, no DOM assumptions, works if you ever add a framework. Cost: every form still
    repeats the read-inputs/show-errors boilerplate, so you've deduplicated half the problem.
  - [1mPure core + optional DOM binder[0m — same core, plus a thin [38;5;153mattachValidation(formEl, schema, onValid)[39m layered on top
    that collects field values by [38;5;153mname[39m, renders messages next to the offending inputs, and calls your handler when
    clean. Forms shrink to a schema and a submit callback. The core stays independently usable, so the binder is opt-in,
    not a lock-in. Cost: the binder makes assumptions about your markup (field naming, where error text goes) that
    you'd have to live with or override.
  - [1mFull form controller[0m — the above plus live revalidation on blur/input, touched-state tracking, submit-button
    disabling. Best UX, but it's a state machine, and it's the piece most likely to fight whatever framework you adopt
    later.

  My recommendation: the middle one, built as two separate layers rather than one thing. The core is where correctness
  lives and it should have no idea the DOM exists; the binder is a convenience over it that you can bypass for any form
  with unusual markup. That split also means the third option stays available later — a controller would sit on top of
  the same core without disturbing it.

  One markup assumption to flag now: the binder needs to find fields by [38;5;153mname[39m attributes, and the current login inputs
  only have [38;5;153mid[39ms ([38;5;153mindex.html:9-10[39m). Adopting the binder means adding [38;5;153mname[39m attributes to those inputs.
